I have both a 21164PC-533 and a PII-400, both running Linux.  For some
things the alpha is clearly faster (the recent DES challenge, some
custom FP intensive software, mp3 encoding, etc.), but overall the
machines seem somewhat comparable.  For purely integer type stuff,
such as LaTeX-ing a large document they are very similar in
performance.

Without having run any specific tests, I think compiling (the kernel
for example) on the alpha is a bit faster.  This may be due to its
better disk subsystem (UW-SCSI).

I used both machines as a combination x-terminal (connecting to other
systems) and general use Unix computer (emacs, LaTeX, Netscape, mh,
etc.)  and they both are ``fast enough'' for that type of stuff.

I finally decided to go with the PII-400 on my desk because of the
increased stability of X.  Though a new X-server for the alpha came
out about 3 days after I gave the alpha up, and X seems stable now.  I
still use the alpha for many CPU intensive tasks, but use the PII-400
for most of my miscellaneous computing needs (email, web, news, etc.).

Unless you have a specific task that is your killer-app it is really
hard to say which is faster.  My comments are for the 21164PC, so the
21164a (LX) is even a bit faster, which will widen the gap even more
between the alpha and a PII.  Some FP stuff is easily twice as fast on
my alpha as on the PII, but for general use, does it really matter if
I can LaTeX a complicated paper in 1.13 seconds or 1.14 seconds?

--
Jeff Lessem.



