On 31 Jul 1998 08:09:29 GMT, G. Hugh SONG <ghsong@PlSNoSpamkjist.ac.kr> wrote:
>> > On 30 Jul 1998 18:02:30 GMT, Christian Stieber wrote:
>> > well, we've got workstations based on a 21164-566 and a 21164-600
>> > running Red Hat 5.0, as well as a PII/300 running Red Hat 5.1.  we
>> > use lots of Fortran, and get pretty poor performance on the alphas.
>> > my codes, for example, run at about the same speed on the PII/300 as
>> > the Alpha/600.  some others around here are a little luckier.
>
>How good is NDP Fortran by Microway?  Is it that poor?

we bought NDP Fortran because Microway gave a price break if we bought
it at the same time we got the machine.  to be honest, i haven't put
it through its paces, but this is because of one fatal (to us) flaw:
it doesn't support INTEGER*8.  we have lots of Fortran code that does
dynamic memory allocation vi calls to C subroutines that do a malloc
for us.  in order to use these routines, we have to pass them a "pointer",
and for us that means an INTEGER of the proper length.  on Intel, it's
INTEGER*4, and on Alpha it's INTEGER*8.  that one shortcoming of NDP
keeps us from using it at all.

other than that, it really looks like a quick-and-dirty port of a 32-bit
compiler.  and there's no on-line documentation.  definitely not a product
with high polish.  in fact, the Microway rep told us "if you want to do
lots of Fortran, you should get DU".

for us, it was a waste of $700.




