On Fri, 31 Jul 1998 17:15:10 +0200, Joachim Wesner wrote:
>Matt Nelson wrote:
>> we bought NDP Fortran because Microway gave a price break if we bought
>> it at the same time we got the machine.  to be honest, i haven't put
>> it through its paces, but this is because of one fatal (to us) flaw:
>> it doesn't support INTEGER*8.  we have lots of Fortran code that does
>> dynamic memory allocation vi calls to C subroutines that do a malloc
>> for us.  in order to use these routines, we have to pass them a "pointer",
>> and for us that means an INTEGER of the proper length.  on Intel, it's
>> INTEGER*4, and on Alpha it's INTEGER*8.  that one shortcoming of NDP
>> keeps us from using it at all.
>> 
>> other than that, it really looks like a quick-and-dirty port of a 32-bit
>> compiler.  and there's no on-line documentation.  definitely not a product
>> with high polish.  in fact, the Microway rep told us "if you want to do
>> lots of Fortran, you should get DU".
>
>This is pretty interesting, if you look at their (MWs) homepage, you
>find a document where they describe their highly sophisticated optimizer
>and scheduler technology especially tuned for the Alpha that tests
>"thousends to millions" code combinations and does highly efficient
>unrolling and "software pipelining" to run tight kernels at the
>theoretical possible rate and and and......

if this is true, then they have their priorities all screwed up.  they
should first implement the basics, THEN tweek it for optimal speed.  the
INTEGER*8 thing is especially agravating, since they actually give an
example in their manual about using a C malloc routine that passes an
INTEGER pointer.  of course this was with 32-bits in mind; you CAN NOT
implement that example on the Alpha.  what kind of craziness is that?

they might have all the stuff you talked about, but what they end up
with is a compiler that is good for little toy problems, at the expense
of 'real' ones.

-matt




