ghsong@PlSNoSpamkjist.ac.kr (G. Hugh SONG) wrote:

> I have a question on this libm thing.  I thought that this libm library the 
most
> critical thing for floating-point calculation.  But not many people mention
> about this.  We are all lamenting the lack of a decent FORTRAN compiler.
> Which is the most critical part, the compiler or libm?

[I'll try to answer this from the egcs (http://egcs.cygnus.com) perspective]

This is a good question and - unfortunately - there is no simple answer to 
it.  It all depends on what computations your main application is doing.

A lot a transcendentals (sin, cos, exp, log, etc ...) were a problem in the 
past on Alpha's.  The standard routines in (g)libc aren't that fast; enter 
Joachim Wesner's work which should render this part a lot better.

For the ordinary "traverse arrays doing +,-,*,/" there's still a short-coming 
(on all architectures egcs supports) that's in the process of being repaired, 
namely the fact that neighboring array elements get assigned separate 
registers:

      do i = 2, n
         b(i) = a(i - 1) + a(i)
      enddo

one would like to have the a(i-1) addressed as Rx(-4) (assuming Rx holds the 
current pointer to a(i)).

