 Christian Stieber <stieber@Informatik.TU-Muenchen.DE> writes:

|> - SCSI: the current Digital boards (164SX, 164LX (which I like most, not
|>   knowing how much I'll have to pay for it...) and 164) don't have onboard
|>   SCSI. Does the EIDE port use DMA? Can Linux _boot_ from a SCSI disk
|>   via some PCI card? Which boards are supported?

I don t know exactly about IDE DMA support, but it seems to be recommended to
use scsi on alpha (at least for the hard drives), since the IDE performances are
no good (mainly under Linux, because of real weak drivers for the chipset from
what I heard).
Yes it can boot from a scsi HD, lots of people have scsi only boxes. The only
thing is that you ve to have a recognized controller. As for myself I boot
MILO from a floppy, since the alphabios has difficulties detecting the disks on
my Tekram UW card, but once I put a 2940 with external drive and it was
detected. There s a list of supported controllers on www.dcginc.com for
instance. Mainly, you can go for the adaptec, but the support under Linux isn t
that great (things are changing I think), or the Intraserver. As for other cards
I don t know. The tekram seems to have pb with alphabios but works great under
linux.


|> - Installing in general: I don't really want to buy Digital Unix or WindowsNT,
|>   so can I _install_ Linux without these? As far as I can see, MILO
|>   can boot Linux right away, but I need to install MILO and some basic
|>   Linux kernel and software first...

The alphabios allow you to launch milo from either a disk partition, a floppy or
a cdrom, so the first you can launch it from floppy or cdrom, then install Linux
and copy it on a disk partition for the next time. You don t need any extra OS
for that. I ve only installed Linux on mine.


|> - PCI: I assume that most of the PCI boards that are around are 32 bit
|>   boards. Can I use these boards in a 64 bit slot?

Yes it works (hopefully, since I ve already 5 pci boards to plug in those only 4
slots... If it didn t work, I ll have to throw away 3 of them!).

|> - PCI, again: assuming I have an x86 Linux driver for soe board,
|>   how "compatible" are these to the Alpha Linux/hardware? Well, rather:
|>   would making an Alpha driver involve a complete rewrite, or can x86
|>   drivers generally be used without "too many" change?

I think there aren t too many changes since the PCI specification should remain
the same. Mainly the problems could stem from the 64bits long pointers vs 32bits
long on Intel. But I m not a device driver expert yet. :o)



