Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
References: <35BDDDA7.F1EB5084@mema.ucl.ac.be> <Pine.OSF.3.96.980729091711.22464A-100000@holly.che.ncsu.edu> <35BF3474.BB301C09@mema.ucl.ac.be> <Pine.OSF.3.96.980729150500.22464I-100000@holly.che.ncsu.edu> <35BF78C3.3194100A@frankfurt.netsurf.de>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
Organization: I.N.R.I.A Rocquencourt
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


Guys,

I think the important point is how the programs are linked.

Stuff that is dynamically linked on Digital Unix requires the shared
object libraries which can only be installed on machines with a
D.U. license.

But stuff that is statically linked needs no libraries and no license.
It will "just run" on Linux unless it uses some particularly esoteric
D.U.-isms.

E.g., Netscape for D.U. is dynamically linked.  No go.
Whereas my own C programs compiled with "-non_shared" work just fine.

Rob.



