Kevin Hudson wrote in message <35BAB62B.80D51BE5@pgs.com>...
>I am going to build a super mondo cool, ass kickin' Linux machine.  I am
>trying to decide If I want to build it around Intel or Alpha.   If I go
>Intel, it will be a dual pentium pro.  300Mhz.  If I go Alpha,  I am
>going to use the 600 Mhz cpu, (Whatever that model num is).   The thing
>is, I can barely spell Alpha, let alone know any thing mor thechnical
>about it.  But I am some what of a hardware hacker, ( in the true sense
>of the word )  and am looking forward to the expeirence.  My problem is
>that I cannot find any bare Alpha mother board sources.  I have found
>plenty of pre assembled machines within the budget that I have allocated
>for this project, but I really want to do this myself.  Does any body
>know where I can go to find 600 Mhz Alpha motherboards with processor?
>I tied the DEC main home page and Microway.  Microway implied that they
>sold motherboards, but all the related links took me to pages for
>preassembled systems.  Any pointers are appreciated.
This isn't going to be popular in comp.os.linux.alpha, but unless you
are doing lots of floating point stuff, I'd say go with Intel. It's worth
noting
btw, that PPro isn't available in 300MHz, only Pentium II. I just recently
picked up a PPro motherboard very cheaply ($89 for Intel's PR440FX
w/support for 2 CPU's, UW SCSI, and 100baseT all on the board). It
worked out pretty well, except I didn't get a very good price on memory
for it.

In general, Linux/Alpha still has some work to go before it's as efficient
with the CPU as Linux/Intel is. The 21164 performs very well with optimized
code, but gcc just isn't there, and egcs is only starting. As a consequence,
I'd expect Alpha would potentially lose a bang for the buck comparision
with integer benchmarks against Intel.

Depending on how forward looking you are, you could take the chance,
as progress is being made as we speak to speed up Alpha performance.
The Beowulf project(s) are probably helping this movement I suspect. One
other thing to keep in mind with Alpha: the 21264 is expected out sometime
during the fall, and with it, I'd expect Alpha pricing to shift.

--Chris





