t.bauer@nowhere.handshake.de (Torsten Bauer) writes:

> But what should I do with the other directories and files? So I get  
> sometimes a lot of failures, like clock skew detected, ...

A partial solution I've used is this:

  log in as `root'
  do this in bash:
    touch /foo; find / -newer /foo \! -type l -exec touch {} \;
    rm /foo

However, this doesn't fix symbolic links, which are purposely
excluded because, if included, touch actually `touches' the
non-symlinks to which they ultimately point, not the links
themselves.  (And you don't necessarily want to do that, though
I did and it seems to have led to no problems.  That is, while
a link might be 2018-dated, the file or directory to which it
points might not be, in which case touching it is not necessary,
and perhaps not appropriate.)

I haven't bothered to find a solution to the problem of fixing
the dates on symbolic links.  It surprises me that GNU `touch'
doesn't offer a --no-dereference option for this, but perhaps
there's no way to do it in UNIX anyway, other than to save the
symbolic-link string, delete the link, then reinstate it -- which
would set the date only to the current date, while touch supports
so much more.  (For that matter, I'd like a --verbose option
as well.)

(Note that if you want more cleverness, you can provide options
to `touch' to set the date/times to something other than current --
in fact, this is arguably better in this case, since 2018 dates
probably correspond to dates *before* when you fixed your
configuration.  Read the docs for more details.)

Disclaimer: If any of the above advice results in your toasting
your system, I'm sorry, but that's about all.  :)
