In article <35B8691D.D92C9059@starlab.net>,
Ivo Clarysse  <soggie@starlab.net> wrote:
>We've got a bunch of headless (no video card installed) DEC PWS500a(u)
>boxes here, on which we're running Linux.  Now, this all works fine
>using SRM and BSD-style partitions; we can set them up via the serial
>port at 9600/8/N/1.

We have a similar setup with our collection of Alpha's, but boot them
via the SRM and bootp.  However, they come shipped with the ARC interface
as the default, so we had to change them without installing monitors
and video cards and keyboards into each one, too.  On our larger order
of Miata's we specified that the SRM be setup at the factory as the
default interface.

>However, if they're setup to boot in AlphaBIOS instead of SRM, and no
>video card is installed, AlphaBIOS uses the serial port as well.  So
>far, so good.  But how can I send [F2], or any function key, to
>AlphaBIOS when using AlphaBIOS  over the serial port ?

This is perhaps the greatest example of brain damage in the ARC setup.
If you boot with the video card and monitor installed BUT NO KEYBOARD,
it will default to the serial port for I/O and display ON THE MONITOR the
keyboard equivilants for the function keys.  IIRC, the function keys
are mapped to the control keys in a linear fashion:

  ^A	F1
  ^B	F2
  ^C	F3
  ^D	F4
  ^E	F5
  ^F	F6
  and so on...

No one at DEC seemed to know this and I encountered it by accident.
Why in the world would they have it display this on the VGA rather
than the (now active) serial console?  Who thought up this absurdity?

Sorry for the rant, but it has bugged me ever since we started using
these machines.  Don't get me started on the Pyxis bugs and other
goofiness that we have come to expect.

Good luck,
Tramm
