Craig Burley <burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> writes:
> I haven't bothered to find a solution to the problem of fixing
> the dates on symbolic links.  It surprises me that GNU `touch'
> doesn't offer a --no-dereference option for this, but perhaps
> there's no way to do it in UNIX anyway [...]

You can read the access/modify/change date and time using stat() or
lstat() depending on whether you want to follow links or not.  You can
write the access/modify date and time using utime() but you can't set
the change time to anything other than "now" nor choose not to follow links.

As far as I know.

:(

I wrote a wee program called 'chyear' for changing access or modify years.
You can do summat like this (in tcsh):

  foreach file ( `find . -mtime -0 -print` )
  chyear -m $file 2018 1998
  end

which changes anything with a modify time in 2018 to 1998 (if you have
permission to change it).

Anyone want?

Rob.
