Christopher B. Browne wrote:

> On Fri, 24 Jul 1998 09:06:49 -0500, Jason Haas <jhaas@linuxppc.org> posted:
> >> Is it possible or likely that RedHat, or one of the other main Intel/Alpha
> >> companies, will formally support the Mac/PPC? Is it likely commercial
> >> software will support Linux on the Mac (e.g. Oracle or Informix)?
> >
> >Re: Red Hat: they've said in the past that they're not interested in
> >PowerPC technology. So, it's up to us right now.
> >
> >As for the big boys... I don't know yet. We're not to the point where we're
> >able to formally approach them.
>
> It's certainly too early to approach "big boys;" I suspect that even
> Alpha is not likely to get heavy attention just yet, and the mix
> *there* will depend on what weird things come out of Merced marketing.
>
> The problem with Linux/PPC is that it's almost a "sole source" product,
> what with the vast abandonment of the PPC market by vendors other than
> Apple and IBM.
>
> I've got a UMAX C/500 box (picked up cheap at an auction) that may soon
> run Linux, but that's a one-off thing that commercial folk certainly
> want no part of.
>
> My argument is that VA Research (related company to UMAX) should be
> selling PPC Linux boxes.  They'd be the most natural vendor.  Barring
> that, you have a choice of:
> a) Hacking something to run on Apple Macs, or
> b) Has anyone gotten an RS/6000 box to run Linux?
>
> I heartily agree with the notion of having further choices beyond
> IA-32 and Alpha; I would *love* to see there be SMP boxes assortedly
> with StrongARM, MIPS, and PPC chips out there.
>
> What these architectures need in order to "take off" is for people to
> be able to buy PCI motherboards for about the same price as an IA-32
> motherboard.
>
> Double the price, while adding slots for a 4- or 8-way SMP selection,
> and they would be similarly (or more) viable in the Linux "marketplace."
>
> Unfortunately, at present, there isn't a market for 100,000 board runs
> for this sort of thing, which is what you need in order to pull the
> price down to near-IA-32 levels.
> --
> [In response to deprecations of the use of <linux/*.h>]  ...I prefer
> code that only compiles on Linux... I really don't care to help other
> operating systems grow.  -- Albert D. Cahalan
> <acahalan@saturn.cs.uml.edu>
> cbbrowne@hex.net -  <http://www.hex.net/~cbbrowne/lsf.html>

  I find it very unfortunate that Linux for PPC isn't moving faster.  I find the
PPC to be a great chip family -- just having a bunch of registers makes it a lot
better than x86 family for my money.  I work a lot in embedded VME systems, and
PPC-based boards are more powerful and priced about the same as 68k-based
boards.  We have been forced to buy expensive RTOS to support these boards, when
in many cases we didn't really need RTOS, but TCP/IP, multi-threading, etc. that
PPC Linux would have been great for.

If a very good implementation was available for G3 Macs, for example, I would be
happy to purchase that hardware if for no other reason than to promote a higher
level of diversity (i.e., to push against the immovable object that is Wintel).




