I found http://www.toast.net/~strycher/AS200.html and 
http://www.mindspring.com/~ccsf-lug/AS200.htm to be very helpful.

In short, get the latest ARC: 
  ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/ALpha/firmware/readmes/alpha200.html

Then get the latest Milo:
  ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/milo-avanti-980606

Get the latest linload.exe (1.5):
  ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Miniloader/alphabios/linload_1.5.exe

You may want to get the latest XL kernel from the same place as milo:
  ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/xl-2030-fixed.gz
(I can't remember if I used this kernel or not).

Update your bios according to DECs instructions.  Then copy linload and milo
to a DOS formatted floppy disk as 'linload.exe' and 'milo'.  Use dd or rawrite
to write the kernel to a floppy.  Set up ARC to boot milo from the floppy with 
a boot menu something like this:

  LOADIDENTIFIER  =Linux
  SYSTEMPARTITION=multi(0)disk(0)fdisk(0)
  OSLOADER=multi(0)disk(0)fdisk(0)\linload.exe
  OSLOADERPARTITION=multi(0)disk(0)fdisk(0)
  OSLOADFILENAME=\milo
  OSLOADOPTIONS=

At the MILO> prompt, insert your kernal disk and type "boot floppy".  
Follow the prompts and you should be fine.  

If you have a 24-bit TGA card, you also have to get Tim Rowley's patched TGA
server.  I grabbed the binary replacement from:
  http://www.cs.brown.edu/~tor/xfree86/XF86_TGA_980706.gz

Gunzip this file and replace /usr/X11R6/bin/XF86_TGA.

Hope this helps.

Richard




