We've got a bunch of headless (no video card installed) DEC PWS500a(u)
boxes here, on which we're running Linux.  Now, this all works fine
using SRM and BSD-style partitions; we can set them up via the serial
port at 9600/8/N/1.

However, if they're setup to boot in AlphaBIOS instead of SRM, and no
video card is installed, AlphaBIOS uses the serial port as well.  So
far, so good.  But how can I send [F2], or any function key, to
AlphaBIOS when using AlphaBIOS  over the serial port ?  I tried hitting
[F2] in Minicom, to no avail.  Typing [Esc]-'['-'['-'B' didn't do
anything either.  The problem is that in order to switch the machine to
SRM from AlphaBIOS, function keys are necessary.  Of course, I _could_
install a temporary video card and a keyboard to switch the box to SRM,
but being able to use the serial port for this would be much simpler.

Another problem with headless AlphaBIOS boots - when no videocard is
installed,  AlphaBIOS (v5.65) will not automatically boot the first
defined OS (I need to type [ENTER] over the serial port).  Is there no
way around this, other than using SRM instead ?



