henke@rocza.krall.org (Douglas G. Henke) writes:
> [ a tale of 2.1.110, "unknown insn", undefined "memsetw" and other
>   assorted annoyances, full of sound and fury, signifying nothing ]

Success. I am now running 2.1.110pre2 (with modules) on my Noname.
Here are the "gotchas" I found (config included at the end):

1) When compiling with egcs 1.0.3a with HAIFA scheduling enabled, the
   compiler bombs with "unknown insn" at the end of any function whose
   body contains a call to __builtin_return_address().

   Since the offending pseudo-function appears in only a few places,
   all of which are arguments to printk(), I snipped it:

   printk("Bombed at %p\n", __builtin_return_address()); becomes
   printk("Bombed someplace.\n");

   This is obviously not the Right Thing (but does work). I think the
   real fix lies in the compiler. If that's long in coming, maybe a
   CONFIG_HAIFA would be a reasonable interim workaround.

2) Compiles OK but link fails with unknown symbols memsetw and memsetw_io.
   Solution: say yes to the framebuffer stuff at config time. (See attached
   config.)

3) Compiles OK but link fails with unknown symbol scroll_ok. Solution:
   snip the two references to the offending symbol.

4) Compiles and links, but can't build coda module support because of
   redundant definition of "proc_sys_root". Solution: change redundant
   definition to external reference.

5) Can't build nvram module on alpha platform. Solution: configure it
   out. (SFX: Duh.)

Once I got everything built, it booted on the first try and seems to
be running fine. One caveat is that it is a headless box (has an ISA
VGA card but no monitor attached) so I have no idea if the console is
reasonable. Another is that many folks have been reporting breakage
of PPP under 2.1.109; this would be a show-stopper if true, but I have
no way to test it right now.

