Dirk Grunwald (grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu) wrote:

(when discussing an ease of installation)

: Give me DU any day.

Just two days ago I wasted many hours when installing DU 4.0D on one box.
After nearly everything was done and a machine was supposed to reboot and
configure itself the whole installation program was crashing.  Once could
boot a resulting mess into a single user mode using genvmunix but it was
scrambled, not usable and I could not find a way to force a configuration.
Talk about a "black box" installation!  Just to make it different from
"not a very good documentation" this is not documented at all.  One of
prescribed steps failed and you are totally screwed.  (Quick: where are
documented all options, and their real meaning, to 'boot'  SRM command?)

For the benefit of all you who may run into this this installation
process does not seem to like too much memory.  With both memory banks
populated it crashes.  With a memory only in one bank it runs and
the resulting system is fine once you installed and inserted "extra"
memory back.  A great fun.

The other "interesting" DU installation occures (this was much
more popular with all versions below 4.0D as graphics support changed
drastically) when one has to install on a machine when you have
only a console (not a serial terminal for whatever reasons), and you
NEED at least a text support on a console, but your graphics card
is not supported by DU.  The installation program attempts to start
X windows anyway and totally locks the whole thing.  Yes, I know
how to work around this "nice feature" but this is not for weak in
heart.

The point is that, unfortunately, you can run into installation troubles
no matter with what.  At least with Linux you can find an information
when you find yourself in a tight spot - even if this information is
hidden in sources.  And if you will try to tell me that W95 always
install easily - do not waste your breath.  I had "fun" with some
W95 machines after which you will take gladly ten Linux installations
in a row on Alpha :-).  The only "fool proof" method is when your
OS comes preinstalled with your new hardware.

I am not trying to defend here 5.1 Red Hat distribution, which is
screwed in some points - AFAIK.  I am only pointing out that all those
who claim "mission impossible" did not see very much yet. :-)

  Michal






