marcelor@acs.bu.edu (Marcelo Rodrigues) writes:
>  I just got an alpha AXPpci33 motherboard and reading the
> documentation I noticed that it requires a 3Volt power supply. The
> board is an AT format but apparently it needs something other than
> an AT power supply. I would like to know where can I get a power
> supply that will work with it.

DEC^H^H Compaq pub EK-AXPCI-DG.B01 (Digital AXPpci33 Alpha AXP PC
Motherboard OEM Design Guide) says:

"The other connector (J34)[1] provides +3.3 volts to the PCI option slots.
[snip]
 Power sequencing of the +3.3V for the AXP processor by the power supply
 is not required. The Alpha AXP processor +3.3V supply is derived from
 +5V and sequenced by an on-board circuit. The +3.3V power connector
 provides power only to the PCI slots."

In other words, get a garden variety (but beefy -- at least 300W) AT
power supply. Plug it into J33. Leave J34 empty and don't worry about it
unless you have some mutant PCI card that won't work without a dedicated
+3.3V supply.

I've tried a few different PCI graphics and ether cards, and haven't yet
found one that required J34 power to work.

[1]-- J34 (the one you don't bother hooking up) is closest to the edge
      of the mobo. J33 (the one you connect to an AT power supply) is
      closer to the center of the board than J34.

Disclaimer: Works for me. May cause your AXPpci33 to ignite the atmosphere
and end all life on the planet. I am not responsible if this happens,
though I promise not to laugh.

-- 
