james@animal.blarg.net (James Hillyerd) wrote:
>Will RedHat Linux work on the following machine:

Yes it will.  I've got one running Redhat 4.2 quite nicely and there are 
numerous reports that Redhat 5.1 works on it as well. This machine has been
sold on www.onsale.com for the last 2 years under various and sundry names. 
The latest is "Web Browser PC". The processor isn't very fast as Alphas go and
the thing is starved for cache. while 256K of cache is fine for low end x86
machines, it kills performance on this machine. Expect to get anywhere from
Pentium 90 to Pentium 120 performance out of depending on what you are doing.
Also note that the the built in video will only do 8bpp which means 256 colors
at most. The good news is that it will do it easily at 1280x1024 and even
1600x1200 if you get the correct modelines (I've done it myself actually).

The box takes 72pin True Parity Simms. Note that most PCs don't use True Parity
memory so you'll have to make sure that you get the right stuff. Its pretty
cheap right as well. I recommend at least 48 MB to get some decent performance.
The binaries for this machine are about a third larger than binaries on x86
machines so you'll need a third more ram to get the same kind of performance.

The built in sound is Microsoft Sound System compatible and works fine.
The PCMCIA slots work as well and there are numerous reports of users
installing 33.6K pcmcia modems in there machines. The PCI slot works but
doesn't give much room. Most folks use this as a way of putting in a video card
that will let them do greater than 8bpp however most card won't fit. There are
reports of some of the Matrox card fitting and there are numerous S3 based
cards that fit and work fine. You will have to recompile the kernel to 
support the video card though.

The bit about running 16/32 windows apps only applies to running WinNT 4.0 on
this baby. You can do that and use FX!32 to run x86 binaries as well. Or you
can use em86 under Linux and run x86 linux binaries under Redhat alpha linux.

Check out the UDB/Multia FAQ for some other interesting info.

hope this helps

V
