bentai@my-dejanews.com writes:
> I am getting an unaligned trap then a segmentation fault when I run man.
> Does this mean I should rebuild man or the kernel?

Seg fault when man is run: This is a known problem in RH5.1. Solution:
get the updated man RPM from the Alpha-specific errata section of the
Red Hat web page:
   http://www.redhat.com/support/docs/rhl/alpha/rh51-errata-alpha.html

Workaround: Specify the full path to the manual page source, e.g.:
"man /usr/man/man1/ls.1" instead of "man ls".

Unaligned trap: Harmless. It means that some program is trying to do a
memory access which isn't naturally aligned, e.g. a word starting at a
memory address that isn't a multiple of 8. Linux catches this and
emulates it in software. The program still runs correctly, albeit more
slowly than it would if it were compiled optimally for your machine.

Solution: Rebuild the offending program (or get a binary RPM where
someone has done so for you).

Workaround: Ignore.

> So far, I like RedHat for the Alpha, but there are some weird stability
> problems that are popping up like this.

Make sure you check the above URL and also:
   http://www.redhat.com/support/docs/rhl/alpha/rh51-errata-general.html

You may find that some of the problems go away. If you are a
developer, it might be a good idea to upgrade to the latest stable
egcs and binutils as well.

The installation instructions seemed to me the weakest part of RH5.1;
it is as if they were mainly just typeset version of HOWTO documents
from the net, most of which haven't been updated in a while.

Other than that, it seems quite good, though in some ways the Alpha
port lags the Intel port. That's only natural, given the relative
numbers of people actively working on and testing each one.

Idle questions, apropos of nothing:

1) Is anyone actively maintaining MILO? Any plans to make it work with
   (e.g.) 2.0.35? I had a stab at this, but it looks non-trivial and I
   don't want to duplicate effort if someone else is already on the case.

2) Has anyone gotten kernel 2.1.x for x >= 100 to compile and run on
   alpha?

