I've just inheirited a brace of DEC AlphaStation 200 4/233s (yay!).  One came
equipped with a nice empty hard drive, so I decided that Redhat Linux/Alpha
was just the thing.  I've been bashing around with Slackware and Redhat for
the i386/486/586 for a bit, and the idea of running Linux full-throttle at 64
bits got me a bit excited...

After a week of experimentation, I finally managed to get a working file
system installed (Many many problems here, as the Network Manager of my local
network believes that FTP is a tool of the devil -- imagine dragging the
entire RHL/Alpha installation tree in to the office one Zip disk at a time...
:o(  ). Having finally gotten the box up and running, I thought that making a
backup would be the order of the day.  Herein lies the problem:

I have attached an 8mm SCSI tape drive (Exabyte) to the Alpha, and hope to
use it to make backups.  Problem is that the out-of-the-box installation
kernel does not include SCSI tape drive support.  I've been wrestling for the
better part of a fortnight trying to compile a working kernel (for the
AVANTI) with ST support built in with no luck whatsoever.  The SCSI adapter
claims to be an NCR 53c810 (see dmesg dump below) and I've attempted to
compile in both 53c7,8xx support and the experimental 53c810 with various
strange results.  In the former case, it claims it cannot find/cannot reset
the adapter and fails to mount the base filesystem (go fig), or in the
latter, it continuously attempts to reset the SCSI bus until you hit the
power switch.... :o(

