X-Deja-AN: 371405663
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <6o8pnm$fpb$1@shiva.direcpc.com> <x790lzu6x6.fsf@rocza.krall.org> <6o9dn2$nol$1@news.ro.com>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: National Institute of Standards and Technology
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Chris Adams wrote:

> According to Douglas G. Henke <henke@rocza.krall.org>:
> >They've got about forty-'leven hunnert fixes for various and sundry
> >things. Not one of them explains why my system thinks it's Wed, Jul 11
> >2018. This Y2K stuff has gotten way out of hand...
>
> Did you tell Red Hat that it was booting from the ARC firmware when you
> were asked for timezone info during install?  It is important, because
> the clock is set differently under the ARC firmware (resulting in the 30
> year difference).  Look in /etc/sysconfig/clock.  Make sure you have the
> line
>
> ARC=true
>
> in it.  This is not an errata, it is just the way the Alpha system
> works.  It has been that way all along.  I think it is in an FAQ
> somewhere.

Also look in /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit - my version reads /etc/sysconfig/clock
and then ignores the ARC flag unless CLOCKMODE==ARC.

The fix: add  line to /etc/sysconfig/clock: CLOCKMODE=ARC .
I had the 20-year problem even with ARC=true; this solved that problem.

Zeen,

