I meant to say: The multigrid subroutine that solves the Poisson equation
does not use any library functions. I of course use Fortran I/O which in
turn uses libraries. But the solver itself does not use any trigonometric
or exponential functions (as would at some stage FFT based solvers).
The algorithm only uses +,-,*, (not even /).  In fact, the algorithm could
run in fixed point (integer) arithmetic. Since some people have asked,
I've put the souce, makefile, and binaries (both DU and linux/g77) on
	ftp://malaga.math.uni.augsburg.de/incoming/ruede/neumann

Multigrid algorithms are the fastest known for this type of problem.
They can be proven to solve the Poisson equation (in a rectangle) in
35 operations per unknwn, independent of the problem size (and not
much more in more general cases). In my program, however, I *over-solve*
the problem for benchmarking purposes.

> P.S.: If you use any trigonometric functions, square root etc. TRY WESNER's
>       libfm (it's worth it's 0$)!!!

I will try it when I have a use for it. At the moment, I am mostly using
DU anyway, for performance reasons.

