Robert Harley <harley@corton.inria.fr> writes:
> [ a nice response to my request for bzip perf metric under different
>   Alpha compilers ]

Thanks to everyone who responded, both on the 'froup and by mail.

Executive summary of what I heard:

1. GNU libm performance sucks relative to the DU libm.
2. Third-party libms exist which work with gcc and perform much better.
   Some of these are free.
3. GNU libc performance may be somewhat lower than DU libc.
4. Few big crunchy programs spend lots of time in libc (other than waiting
   for I/O).
5. Programs that miss cache a lot may have memory access dominate the
   figures for CPU time spent. This can obscure differences in the
   quality of code generated by different compilers. (Bzip is such
   a program.)
6. egcs 1.0.3x tends to perform incrementally better than gcc 2.[78].x.
7. egcs w/HAIFA scheduling tends to perform incrementally better than
   the same egcs without.
8. All of the above are general trends which may be untrue given
   certain specific (but not necessarily pathological) combinations
   of program and input.

