Richard Griswold <griswold@rconnect.com> writes:
> I have an AlphaStation 200 4/233 and I'm having problems installing
> RedHat 5.1 Alpha on it.

<VOICE MODE="Gomer Pyle">
Surprise, surprise, surprise!
</VOICE>

>   ncr53c810-0-<0,0>: FAST-10 SCSI 10.0 MB/s (100 ns, offset 8)
>   sda : unsupported sector size 520. <<<<<--- !!! WTF? !!!
>   scsi : deleting disk entry.

That's doubleplusungood. I don't have a solution, but I admire the
problem. I suspect cabling, termination or termination power issues,
though, simply on the basis of the fact that it's SCSI.

Your drive should indeed be 512 bytes per "sector." (The sectors are
more of a conceptual nicety than a physical reality. My drive geometry
on a 4GB drive reads 133 heads, 62 sectors, 1017 cylinders. I'm pretty
darn sure the drive doesn't have 67 or more physical platters.)

> According to RedHat, the NCR 53C8xx SCSI controller has "Tier 2"
> compatibility, meaning that it is partially supported.  Am I running
> into a compatibility problem?

I don't think the chipset itself is the problem. I have an AXPpci33
with the same SCSI chipset, running one CD-ROM and one hard drive.
(Much like your system, only the drive is a Micropolis.) It seems to
work like a champ.

Here's some choice output from /proc/scsi/scsi and /proc/scsi/ncr53c8xx/0:

General information:
  Chip NCR53C810, device id 0x1, revision id 0x1
  IO port address 0x8800, IRQ number 11
  Synchronous period factor 25, max commands per lun 4
Profiling information: [snipped]
Attached devices: 
Host: scsi0 Channel: 00 Id: 00 Lun: 00
  Vendor: MICROP   Model: 3243-19   1128RV Rev: 28RV
  Type:   Direct-Access                    ANSI SCSI revision: 02
Host: scsi0 Channel: 00 Id: 05 Lun: 00
  Vendor: NEC      Model: CD-ROM DRIVE:502 Rev: 2.0y
  Type:   CD-ROM                           ANSI SCSI revision: 02

> What do I do now?

Double check cabling and termination. Is the _last_ device on the bus
(i.e. the one physically furthest along the cable from the host
adapter) supplying termination? Is it the _only_ thing doing so? Does
it supply termination power? Does a different (shorter?) cable work?
Do you have a different drive you can try? Does the drive you have
work OK in another box?

You're sure the black goat you sacrificed to make your SCSI chain work
was a virgin, right?

Odd thought: maybe the kernel you have (avanti-mumble.gz, right?) has
different -- and broken -- NCR53C810 drivers than the noname-udb
kernel I'm using? Maybe compiling a new AS 200 4/* kernel from up-to-
date sources would help. 

-- 
