I think it should work fine, the Elsa QS1000-PCI is a very good card;
with a few conditions:

- you need the CVS version for isdn4linux, as the standard released
  version has the "standard" bugs about sizeof(int) != sizeof(void *)
- I had trouble with compiling the kernel, gcc crashed with an "internal
  compiler error" in the ISDN sources, and egcs crashed with the same
  error in the memory management stuff. I used 2.1.97, and that at least
  I could compile with egcs.
- egcs still made errors with optimizations, eg:

    copy_to_user(ptr, s, strlen(s) + 1);

  generates a SIGSEGV because with s == "ippp0", the third parameter to
  the function is 36 or something like that... This means "isdnctrl
  addif ippp0" gives a segmentation violation, but it has worked anyway.
  Doing

    int x = strlen(s) + 1;
    copy_to_user(ptr, s, x);

  is a workaround, but it works (this is in isdn_common.c, where
  "case ????AIF:").
  But maybe the compilers nowadays work properly?

>I can also get an ISND PCI ASUS board "cheaply" (based on siemens chip), any
>success stories of those adapters in multia?

I haven't heard of the PCI boards being supported (yet).


