Alex Kent <alex@lanl.gov> writes:

> Having some trouble compiling some software that requires some
> functionality that used to be present in libc5 but now seems to be gone
> in libc6, specifically on under Linux-AXP. (I checked on a libc6 intel
> box and it was missing there as well)
> 
> The following existed in glob.h under a libc5 system but is gone under
> libc6:
> #if !defined (_POSIX_C_SOURCE) || _POSIX_C_SOURCE < 2 || defined (_GNU_SOURCE)
> /* If they are not NULL, `glob' uses these functions to read directories.  */
> extern __ptr_t (*__glob_opendir_hook) __P ((__const char *__directory));
> extern __const char *(*__glob_readdir_hook) __P ((__ptr_t __stream));
> extern void (*__glob_closedir_hook) __P ((__ptr_t __stream));
> #endif
> 
> 
> Does anyone know what happened here and how I can get similar
> functionality under libc6?

The glob.h used in libc5 was very old and the interface to this
extended functionality was bad.  It isn't thread safe.

See the definition of glob_t in the glibc glob.h header and you'll see
that there are similar elements to these variables and this interface
is thread-safe.

