On Sat, 27 Jun 1998, Robb wrote:

> or SRPMS. I have RTFM and whatnot, but cannot figure out the correct way
> to install a SRPM. Would someone be kind enough to give me the procedure
> and commands, or a pointer to some information?

What I do is rpm -i the RPM, and then rebuild it using the "rpm -ba
/path/to/spec-file" command (the files will be unpacked in
/usr/src/redhat).  See if you have a /usr/doc/rpm-ver.num directory.
Another thing you can do is use rpm2cpio to conver the RPM to a cpio
archive, then install that in a temp directory and build it by hand.  I
used to do this before I ran a RedHat system.

> 	Also, I have downloaded the latest egcs snapshot, and would like to
> install it as a secondary compiler. What is the method to install it
> without blasting away the existing gcc? What invocation to use when
> compiling with egcs? Again, instructions or a link would be much
> appreciated.

I installed my egcs in /usr/local (using ../configure --prefix=/usr/local
--with-local-prefix=/usr/local
--with-gxx-include-dir=/usr/local/include/g++) and then installed it.  I
then either set /usr/local/bin before /usr/bin in my search path (not
ideal) or set up symlinks in my ~/bin called egcs and egcs++ that point to
the binaries in /usr/local/bin/gcc/++.

Hope this helps,
-bp
