>anyone tried replacing the original disk with a 2.5" EIDE disk ? I'm
>interested in all experience you can share ...

Yes, you can do this. However, the IDE controller on the UDB/Multia is not an
EIDE controller so the performance is less than stellar. IN fact, any disk I/O
will consume a large majority of your CPU time. I seem to recall situations
where users who had installed 2.5" IDE drives were finding 80% - 90% of the cpu
was being used when copying a 1MB file. This is totally unacceptable for me
personally.

There is currently no manufacturer making 2.5" scsi hard drives. My suggestion
is to use the external scsi connection on your machine (if you have one) to
connect an external scsi drive. It gives you much better performance even
though the price is higher. 

