see Alpha and Linux:

DEC Unix would be far more widely used if the combination of the Unix and
compiler tools didn't cost so much. For the past few years it has seemed
like DEC has been sending the message "Use NT and forget Unix". However,
MSVC v5 has lagged the DEC Unix C/C++ compiler's performance so that hasn't
been an ideal solution (and not ideal for other reasons as well).

DEC has not really tried to court developers (if it feels otherwise it is
quite wrong). That DEC would have spent so much money on Alpha and then not
made Alpha development tools attractive to try out is something that just
amazes me.

Regarding Alpha and Linux: Given that DEC Unix is a rather expensive choice
for a developer (there are companies who charge developers less in order to
get more stuff written for their platforms; why not Compaq/DEC?) lots more
people would be using the Alpha Linux combination if only the gcc compiler
produced faster code. As it is the gcc compiler is producing slower code
than MSVC which is producing slower code than the C/C++ compiler that is
available for DEC Unix.

Back in the heyday of DEC in the 1980s  it seemed like it really understood
the technical market it sold into (I say this as an ex-VAX/VMS programmer
from the aerospace industry). Now DEC could really aim their products at the
scientific and engineering markets if they just tried a little harder to fix
certain areas. But year after year goes by and they do not fix these obvious
areas.

On Linux and NT the Alpha has part of its superior thru-put wasted on weak
compilers. Well, the whole appeal of Alpha is superior thru-put. Wasting
that on weak compilers seems a tragic waste to me (and to not a few others I
suspect).

On DEC Unix the prices for OS and development tools software are too high
compared to the alternatives. Also, DEC Unix is hardly marketed strongly to
the programming masses.

On Alpha NT there is no single focused area of available 3rd party software
to make Alpha NT really useful for any one scientific or engineering area.
National Instruments hasn't ported LabWindows and their other lab stuff.
MatLab isn't available (unless all this has changed since the last time I
checked). Ditto for similar apps in this area.

For client/server you don't have native versions of either the major Win32
RAD tools (PowerBuilder, Delphi, etc) or the major Java development tools
(IBM VAJ, J Builder, etc). Microsoft hasn't even made a native Alpha version
of MS Access.

BTW, I'd love to see Borland/Inprise and/or Sybase port their development
tools to _any_ of the Alpha-based OS's. A really fast Java for Alpha on all
3 OS's that was totally compliant with Sun's spec would help in that regard.

I'd really like to be able to use Alpha for more things. There are several
areas where it could be improved to make it good enough to use in those
areas. So far I see no signs that any of these areas are going to be fixed.
DEC's strategy has so far escaped my understanding. I suspect that I'm not
alone in this...

I would appreciate it if you could forward this message to whoever might
benefit from reading it.






