I'll answer George's question below, but first a plea of my own: I
have an AXPpci33 board with _no cache at all_. As you can imagine,
performance is rather lackluster. I'd be ever so grateful if anyone
could point me at a reliable source of cache. (I'd prefer 128Kx8 15ns,
but I'll settle for 32Kx8 and/or 20ns.)

dowdingg@saturn.math.uaa.alaska.edu (George A. Dowdingg) writes:
> I have just purchaced a ``new'' mother board.  It is an alpha 166 mhz
> [ major snippage ]
> Am I wasting my time with this board, or is it worthwhile to build a
> cheap (hopefully) desktop system around it?

You can indeed build a system around your board. What I expect you
have is one of the AXPpci33 "NoName" boards, with a 21066 Alpha
cpu. (They also take the 233 MHz version, if I remember right.)

Comes in two versions which differ only in that one has a 5-pin DIN
AT-style keyboard connector and the other has PS/2-style keyboard and
mouse mini-DIN ports.

3 ISA, 3 PCI, integrated IDE and SCSI.

I bought one of these a while ago from a FOAF, and it works fine.
Stuck a SCSI drive from onsale.com on it, and dug a case, power
supply, old 4x SCSI CD-ROM and floppy drive out of the spare parts
bin. Had to buy fans, though: lots of fans. Big ones. Mmmm, Alpha
_like_ airflow.

Runs very nicely, it does, modulo the lack of cache I bemoan above.

I was told (by the FOAF) that the AXPpci33 does _not_ like 8MB SIMMs.
I have no idea if this is true; I used a pair of 16MB SIMMs, and they
work fine. It _definitely needs_ 60ns true parity memory, though.

To find out for sure what you have, and for all sorts of good Alpha info,
check:
        http://www.azstarnet.com/~axplinux/

I wrote a detailed account of how I got Red Hat Linux 5.1 installed on
mine. It is with the greatest contrition and shame that I implore you
to avoid my wretched web page: http://www.c-com.net/~henke/alpha/

Happy hacking.
-- 
