If you can point me towards some easy to compile code, I would not
mind building it up with DU C and gcc and comparing the results.

In my own experience, I have found that for C programs some things are
faster with DU C, some things are faster with egcs/gcc, and some
things are the same with either compiler.  I suppose this also has to
do with libm and libc issues.  Some programs might also be bound by
cache and memory issues, so as long as the compiler and libraries can
hit the ceiling rate, they all will be the same speed.

Here are the results of the BYTEmark benchmark with three different
compilers:

BYTEmark* Native Mode Benchmark ver. 2 (10/95)
Index-split by Andrew D. Balsa (11/97)
Linux/Unix* port by Uwe F. Mayer (12/96,11/97)

				Iterations/sec.
TEST                : gcc         : DU C        : egcs HAIFA
                    : 2.7.2.3     : (cc.alt)    : 1.0.3a
--------------------:-------------:-------------:------------
NUMERIC SORT        :     157.99  :      171.2  :     162.84
STRING SORT         :     17.461  :     17.977  :     18.158
BITFIELD            : 5.7397e+07  : 6.1158e+07  : 5.7238e+07
